\ Bounded-memory regression for a local that has the name of a global word.
\
\ `bump` is defined inside `user` and calls the global `count`, so the code compiled for it bakes that
\ word in. `other` binds a local named `count` in its own body. That frame is unrelated to `bump`,
\ since a body's names resolve lexically, so the local cannot shadow anything for it. Shared code was
\ revoked and compiled again whenever any frame defined a name it had baked in, which left a code
\ buffer behind on every call. The loop below stays flat now.

count: ( n -- n ) [ 1 + ] ;

user: ( -- n ) [
  bump: ( n -- n ) [ count 0 + ] ;
  0 101 [ bump ] times
] ;

other: ( k -- n ) [
  count: swap ;
  count
] ;

300 [ user drop 1 other drop ] times

"flat" print-line
